WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:03.960
Now that we have a basic overview of, you know,

2
00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:06.120
the different types of animation in the engine,

3
00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:07.440
how to preview them,

4
00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:11.039
and a very basic understanding of the flow of logic

5
00:00:11.039 --> 00:00:13.240
of how the animations might be triggered,

6
00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:14.400
we're going to jump into the engine

7
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:17.100
and take a look at creating our own animation.

8
00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:20.359
So for that, we'll be using Sequencer and Control Rig.

9
00:00:20.359 --> 00:00:22.359
And we'll also see how we can attach something

10
00:00:22.359 --> 00:00:24.519
to our character and save this out

11
00:00:24.519 --> 00:00:28.390
as a new animation for your project.

12
00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:31.010
We'll be working in Sequencer to create our animation.

13
00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:32.149
So this is where you'll be working

14
00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:33.950
if you want to create a new animation

15
00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:36.549
or edit an existing one in engine.

16
00:00:36.549 --> 00:00:39.990
Now Sequencer is the cinematic tool of Unreal Engine.

17
00:00:39.990 --> 00:00:41.490
And you can use this for creating

18
00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:44.590
and previewing any cinematic sequences that you might have.

19
00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:47.270
So you can add your characters, your cameras,

20
00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:49.189
your effects and lighting.

21
00:00:49.189 --> 00:00:50.950
You can even set up different events and logic

22
00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:52.549
to trigger in the sequence.

23
00:00:52.549 --> 00:00:55.429
Basically just about anything you need for your cinematic,

24
00:00:55.429 --> 00:00:57.270
which you can play in real time,

25
00:00:57.270 --> 00:00:58.789
or you can even render out a video

26
00:00:58.789 --> 00:01:00.869
to use outside of the engine.

27
00:01:00.869 --> 00:01:03.710
Now we do have dedicated courses for Sequencer.

28
00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:05.590
So if there's something you want to learn a bit more about,

29
00:01:05.590 --> 00:01:07.030
then definitely check those out

30
00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:08.109
because we're only really gonna

31
00:01:08.109 --> 00:01:10.030
scratch the surface today.

32
00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:11.170
So for now, we're going to look

33
00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:13.750
at adding a character to the sequence,

34
00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:16.010
see how you can animate with Control Rig,

35
00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:18.609
and then blend some existing animations together

36
00:01:18.609 --> 00:01:20.129
to create a new animation

37
00:01:20.129 --> 00:01:21.409
and see how we can save that out

38
00:01:21.409 --> 00:01:22.730
as its own animation sequence

39
00:01:22.730 --> 00:01:26.219
that you can use in the project.

40
00:01:26.219 --> 00:01:27.319
So first thing we want to do

41
00:01:27.319 --> 00:01:29.200
is create our level sequence.

42
00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:30.439
So we find just an empty space

43
00:01:30.439 --> 00:01:32.760
in your content browser and right click,

44
00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:34.640
come up to cinematics,

45
00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:37.230
then choose level sequence.

46
00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:38.769
And then it'll ask you to rename it.

47
00:01:38.769 --> 00:01:43.640
So I'll just call it my level sequence.

48
00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:46.540
And we can just double click this to open it up.

49
00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:49.150
And this is our Sequencer window right here.

50
00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:52.189
Now this is where you'd be adding your characters

51
00:01:52.189 --> 00:01:53.370
or your lighting, your effects,

52
00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:56.069
whatever it might be that you need for your sequence.

53
00:01:56.069 --> 00:01:57.590
And to add stuff to your sequence,

54
00:01:57.590 --> 00:01:59.750
we can do that in two different ways.

55
00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:02.849
So we could hit the add button here

56
00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:05.129
and choose add actor to sequence.

57
00:02:05.129 --> 00:02:06.569
And what that will do is it'll filter out

58
00:02:06.569 --> 00:02:07.969
everything that you've got

59
00:02:07.969 --> 00:02:10.129
in your current level that you had open

60
00:02:10.129 --> 00:02:12.710
and allow you to add it to your sequence.

61
00:02:12.710 --> 00:02:13.949
Now what this does,

62
00:02:13.949 --> 00:02:15.509
it will create a hard reference

63
00:02:15.509 --> 00:02:16.930
between the sequence and the level

64
00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:18.729
and connect the two of them together.

65
00:02:18.729 --> 00:02:20.930
So that next time you open this level,

66
00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:22.909
or rather next time you open the sequence,

67
00:02:22.909 --> 00:02:26.099
you will also need to have the level open.

68
00:02:26.099 --> 00:02:28.639
Now, the other way we can add something to the sequence

69
00:02:28.639 --> 00:02:31.479
is by just grabbing it from the content browser

70
00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:33.719
and dragging it into the sequence.

71
00:02:33.719 --> 00:02:36.159
So that's what I'm gonna do in this case,

72
00:02:36.159 --> 00:02:38.439
because what it does is it means

73
00:02:38.439 --> 00:02:39.800
when you open the sequence,

74
00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:42.719
it will spawn that asset that I've just dragged in

75
00:02:42.719 --> 00:02:45.240
and it'll reference it from the content browser

76
00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:48.500
rather than from the actual level that you have open.

77
00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:50.539
So that means we could use this sequence

78
00:02:50.539 --> 00:02:54.740
in any other level that we have in the engine.

79
00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:58.340
Now, notice when we dragged our character into the sequence

80
00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:02.139
our actual layout mode changed inside the engine here.

81
00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:04.860
So with this dropdown menu, we have a few different modes

82
00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:08.580
and essentially they are different layouts

83
00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:13.460
that give us access to, I guess, context sensitive

84
00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:15.020
windows and tools that are available

85
00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:16.439
for the different modes.

86
00:03:16.439 --> 00:03:18.460
So when you add a character to a sequence,

87
00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:21.409
it automatically puts you into animation mode.

88
00:03:21.409 --> 00:03:23.210
Now this will give you access to, you know,

89
00:03:23.210 --> 00:03:24.490
some animation friendly tools

90
00:03:24.490 --> 00:03:25.650
that you're probably familiar with

91
00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:27.409
if you've animated before.

92
00:03:27.409 --> 00:03:29.949
So we've got things like poses and tweens

93
00:03:29.949 --> 00:03:31.590
and snapper up here.

94
00:03:32.069 --> 00:03:33.629
So if you want to save any poses for your character,

95
00:03:33.629 --> 00:03:36.610
you can do that with the poses tool.

96
00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:38.729
You can then tween your poses as well

97
00:03:38.729 --> 00:03:39.969
with this little tool down here

98
00:03:39.969 --> 00:03:43.120
and change how the tweener works.

99
00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:44.319
You've also got the snapper.

100
00:03:44.319 --> 00:03:48.159
So this will allow you to snap two objects together

101
00:03:48.159 --> 00:03:50.740
or snap one object to the location,

102
00:03:50.740 --> 00:03:53.599
the transforms of another.

103
00:03:53.599 --> 00:03:56.500
You can see motion trails here as well.

104
00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:59.539
And you've got all the different motion trail settings

105
00:03:59.539 --> 00:04:01.349
that you could edit in here.

106
00:04:01.349 --> 00:04:02.810
You've also got animation layers.

107
00:04:03.370 --> 00:04:04.689
I'm just going to turn that off,

108
00:04:04.689 --> 00:04:08.569
which is a relatively new addition

109
00:04:08.569 --> 00:04:10.689
in the animation mode tab here.

110
00:04:10.689 --> 00:04:12.370
So if you've got any layers that you want to add

111
00:04:12.370 --> 00:04:14.169
when you're animating, you would do that through here.

112
00:04:14.169 --> 00:04:16.279
And this is how you manage them as well.

113
00:04:16.279 --> 00:04:17.779
And you've also got a really handy tool here

114
00:04:17.779 --> 00:04:19.319
that creates a temporary pivot

115
00:04:19.319 --> 00:04:21.079
for whatever you have selected.

116
00:04:21.079 --> 00:04:23.519
So if you want to change where something sort of rotates

117
00:04:23.519 --> 00:04:26.860
from, this is a really useful tool for that.

118
00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:29.439
You also see that we've got the animation outliner here.

119
00:04:29.439 --> 00:04:32.480
So as you would in your, you know, in mayo or blender,

120
00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:35.420
you can select your controls from this window here.

121
00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:37.139
And you can see the animation details

122
00:04:37.139 --> 00:04:38.480
just on the right hand side

123
00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:40.500
for anything you've got selected.

124
00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:44.720
So inside the sequencer window, we have our character

125
00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:46.519
and just below our character,

126
00:04:46.519 --> 00:04:48.759
we've got one of the control rigs

127
00:04:48.759 --> 00:04:50.759
that came in automatically with it.

128
00:04:50.759 --> 00:04:54.000
So if I was to open that and select any of the assets,

129
00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:55.680
you can see that in the details panel here,

130
00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:58.579
whatever we've got selected updates.

131
00:04:58.579 --> 00:05:01.259
So I'm just going to dock our sequencer window here

132
00:05:01.259 --> 00:05:04.100
so that we can see our character in the viewport here

133
00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:05.959
and essentially start animating.

134
00:05:05.959 --> 00:05:08.339
It's really that easy to get started.

135
00:05:08.339 --> 00:05:10.379
So we can grab our controls here

136
00:05:10.379 --> 00:05:14.279
and start posing our character up if we want to.

137
00:05:14.279 --> 00:05:16.899
Now, when you're animating in sequencer,

138
00:05:16.899 --> 00:05:19.459
there are two very useful things to know.

139
00:05:19.459 --> 00:05:21.839
So that would be how to set a key

140
00:05:21.839 --> 00:05:24.079
and then also how to turn on auto key

141
00:05:24.079 --> 00:05:26.339
so that every time you make a change,

142
00:05:26.339 --> 00:05:29.699
sequence is going to set a key for you automatically.

143
00:05:29.699 --> 00:05:32.160
So we can set a key in two different ways.

144
00:05:32.519 --> 00:05:34.879
We could select any of our controls here

145
00:05:34.879 --> 00:05:36.800
and we could set a little plus that adds a key

146
00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:39.759
at the current time,

147
00:05:39.759 --> 00:05:41.300
or you can actually just use S,

148
00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:42.879
which is the shortcut on the keyboard.

149
00:05:42.879 --> 00:05:46.310
So I could select several here and press S

150
00:05:46.310 --> 00:05:48.610
and then drag the timeline along, press S again,

151
00:05:48.610 --> 00:05:49.810
and you'll see that adds new keys

152
00:05:49.810 --> 00:05:52.019
for the ones we have selected.

153
00:05:52.019 --> 00:05:56.079
Now, auto key is just this little blue icon here.

154
00:05:56.079 --> 00:05:58.319
So currently for us, it's turned on.

155
00:05:58.319 --> 00:06:00.120
So if I was to come over here,

156
00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:02.759
select this upper arm and make a change,

157
00:06:02.759 --> 00:06:05.680
you'll see that we automatically get a new key.

158
00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:07.639
That's turned off.

159
00:06:07.639 --> 00:06:09.920
That's not going to happen when I make a change.

160
00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:12.040
And what happens if I scrub through,

161
00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:14.360
you'll see that that change isn't kept.

162
00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:17.189
It only remembers the previous key.

163
00:06:17.189 --> 00:06:18.910
So that may or may not be on by default.

164
00:06:18.910 --> 00:06:21.629
So if it's not, be sure to just toggle it on.

165
00:06:21.629 --> 00:06:23.550
And then anytime you make a change,

166
00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:26.629
it'll save that there for you and set a new key.

167
00:06:26.629 --> 00:06:29.720
I'm just going to highlight these and delete them.

168
00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:32.060
Now to set a key for all of the controls

169
00:06:32.060 --> 00:06:32.899
in our control rig,

170
00:06:32.899 --> 00:06:35.279
I'm just going to scroll back up to the top

171
00:06:35.279 --> 00:06:38.879
and on the name of the control rig essentially here,

172
00:06:38.879 --> 00:06:40.399
I'm just going to hit,

173
00:06:40.399 --> 00:06:42.639
tell you what, let's do it at frame zero.

174
00:06:42.639 --> 00:06:43.779
Just going to hit the plus

175
00:06:43.779 --> 00:06:45.759
to add a new key at the current time.

176
00:06:45.759 --> 00:06:47.639
And you see that as we scrub down,

177
00:06:47.639 --> 00:06:49.319
that's added a key for everything we've got

178
00:06:49.319 --> 00:06:53.629
or every control that we have in the control rig.

179
00:06:53.629 --> 00:06:55.550
So let's make a quick animation here

180
00:06:55.550 --> 00:06:58.430
of our mannequin just waving.

181
00:06:58.430 --> 00:06:59.750
So I'm going to scrub along a little bit

182
00:06:59.750 --> 00:07:02.279
because I've got auto key turned on,

183
00:07:02.279 --> 00:07:05.899
just means that I can scrub along

184
00:07:05.899 --> 00:07:10.519
and grab the controls and just make a new pose.

185
00:07:10.519 --> 00:07:16.720
So he's going to bring his arm up here

186
00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:21.350
and then if I scrub along a little further,

187
00:07:21.350 --> 00:07:32.360
bring it up a little higher,

188
00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:40.319
maybe even bring the clavicle up a little bit

189
00:07:40.319 --> 00:07:42.040
and he's going to wave.

190
00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:45.600
So currently my manipulator handle,

191
00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:48.939
my rotation control here, my gizmo,

192
00:07:48.939 --> 00:07:51.279
is set into object mode.

193
00:07:51.279 --> 00:07:54.639
So we can change that or local space rather.

194
00:07:55.079 --> 00:07:56.240
We can change that to world space

195
00:07:56.240 --> 00:07:59.209
with this icon just up here.

196
00:07:59.209 --> 00:08:01.189
So I know this is breaking the elbow,

197
00:08:01.189 --> 00:08:02.410
but just from our perspective,

198
00:08:02.410 --> 00:08:04.050
I'm going to do that so it's a little easier

199
00:08:04.050 --> 00:08:07.480
for us to get this wave done.

200
00:08:07.480 --> 00:08:08.459
So you can just click this

201
00:08:08.459 --> 00:08:13.920
and toggle between local or world space.

202
00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:16.689
So if we come across here

203
00:08:16.689 --> 00:08:24.040
and then go left and right and left,

204
00:08:24.040 --> 00:08:27.649
we've got a little wave for our character.

205
00:08:27.649 --> 00:08:28.490
And then what I'll do

206
00:08:28.490 --> 00:08:30.790
is I'll get him to bring his hand back down.

207
00:08:30.829 --> 00:08:35.059
So if I select all the controls that we've animated,

208
00:08:35.059 --> 00:08:39.120
I'm just going to press S to set a key to keep this pose.

209
00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:43.179
And then I'm going to highlight these first keys,

210
00:08:43.179 --> 00:08:45.620
press control C to copy them,

211
00:08:45.620 --> 00:08:47.840
and then drag over to our last frame,

212
00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:52.049
press control V and it'll paste those in.

213
00:08:52.049 --> 00:08:55.789
Oh, actually that's not the case for the clavicle,

214
00:08:55.789 --> 00:08:56.629
is it?

215
00:08:56.629 --> 00:08:58.129
Control C, control V.

216
00:08:58.129 --> 00:09:01.860
Yeah, it seems like I forgot to select that one.

217
00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:05.470
Oh, forgot to copy paste that one.

218
00:09:05.470 --> 00:09:07.350
So as you can see now, as we scrub through,

219
00:09:07.350 --> 00:09:11.220
the hand comes up, does a wave and goes back down.

220
00:09:11.220 --> 00:09:18.340
If I just play that, we can see our character waving.

221
00:09:18.340 --> 00:09:19.980
Let's say we were happy with this animation

222
00:09:19.980 --> 00:09:23.320
and we want to save it out as its own animation sequence.

223
00:09:23.320 --> 00:09:25.299
So we're not relying on the sequencer to play it

224
00:09:25.299 --> 00:09:27.659
and the control rig that we have here.

225
00:09:27.659 --> 00:09:29.259
And then once we've got that,

226
00:09:29.259 --> 00:09:31.299
we can use that in any sort of animation logic

227
00:09:31.299 --> 00:09:33.899
that we might have to trigger this animation

228
00:09:34.820 --> 00:09:38.240
during the game or whenever we want to trigger it.

229
00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:40.320
So if I scrub back to the top of our sequence

230
00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:43.720
and select our character mesh, our skeletal mesh mani,

231
00:09:43.720 --> 00:09:47.840
I'll right click and choose bake animation sequence.

232
00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:50.000
Now this will just ask you where you want to save it

233
00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:52.039
and what do you want to call it?

234
00:09:52.039 --> 00:09:54.580
And we'll just call it A underscore wave

235
00:09:54.580 --> 00:09:59.919
and I'll just put it in the content browser base folder.

236
00:09:59.919 --> 00:10:03.279
And that also gives us some extra options

237
00:10:03.279 --> 00:10:05.399
to either tick or untick when we're saving this.

238
00:10:06.200 --> 00:10:07.299
This will be different every time,

239
00:10:07.299 --> 00:10:09.379
depending on what you've got on your character.

240
00:10:09.379 --> 00:10:12.000
So if you've got more targets you want to export with it

241
00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:14.679
or any curves on your bones that you want to export,

242
00:10:14.679 --> 00:10:17.080
generally you can probably just leave this as default,

243
00:10:17.080 --> 00:10:19.419
just hit export animation sequence.

244
00:10:19.419 --> 00:10:24.340
And if we go and find it in our content browser

245
00:10:24.340 --> 00:10:25.860
that we'll click that to open up,

246
00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:29.629
you can see that our animation is playing here.

247
00:10:29.629 --> 00:10:31.509
Saved as its own animation sequence

248
00:10:31.509 --> 00:10:32.629
and we don't need that sequence

249
00:10:32.629 --> 00:10:37.870
or the control rig to play this.

250
00:10:37.909 --> 00:10:41.070
So now that we know how to create a new animation,

251
00:10:41.070 --> 00:10:42.789
let's take a look at how we might be able

252
00:10:42.789 --> 00:10:45.269
to either edit an existing animation

253
00:10:45.269 --> 00:10:47.549
or maybe blend some animations together

254
00:10:47.549 --> 00:10:50.889
to again, create a new animation.

255
00:10:50.889 --> 00:10:53.090
So inside our sequence,

256
00:10:53.090 --> 00:10:56.190
I'm just going to delete our control rig

257
00:10:56.190 --> 00:10:58.690
because if you have a control rig and animations

258
00:10:58.690 --> 00:11:02.090
for a character in a sequence at the same time,

259
00:11:02.090 --> 00:11:03.970
the control rig is going to take priority

260
00:11:03.970 --> 00:11:04.789
and you're not going to be able

261
00:11:04.789 --> 00:11:06.840
to see what animation is playing.

262
00:11:06.840 --> 00:11:09.320
Unless of course you're using a layered control rig,

263
00:11:09.320 --> 00:11:12.320
which we'll touch on in just a few minutes.

264
00:11:12.320 --> 00:11:14.000
So I'm going to right click this

265
00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:15.419
and just press delete.

266
00:11:15.419 --> 00:11:16.820
And you see that a character goes back

267
00:11:16.820 --> 00:11:20.769
to their default pose.

268
00:11:20.769 --> 00:11:24.070
So underneath our Manny character here,

269
00:11:24.070 --> 00:11:25.929
we have an animation track.

270
00:11:25.929 --> 00:11:27.230
Now, if that's something

271
00:11:27.230 --> 00:11:29.870
that's not automatically populated,

272
00:11:29.870 --> 00:11:33.149
you could hit this little plus on your character track.

273
00:11:33.149 --> 00:11:34.870
You could go to the animation tab here

274
00:11:34.870 --> 00:11:38.220
and you could just find an animation and just add that in.

275
00:11:39.059 --> 00:11:41.559
So I could just choose idle, for example,

276
00:11:41.559 --> 00:11:43.820
and that would give us a new animation track.

277
00:11:43.820 --> 00:11:46.610
And then the idle animation is just playing.

278
00:11:46.610 --> 00:11:48.210
So I'm just going to delete this other one.

279
00:11:48.210 --> 00:11:51.970
You could also add the plus on this animation here

280
00:11:51.970 --> 00:11:53.570
and that'll give you the same options.

281
00:11:53.570 --> 00:11:56.720
So it could pick something else

282
00:11:56.720 --> 00:11:58.360
and you can actually see that

283
00:11:58.360 --> 00:12:00.000
you can have multiple animation tracks

284
00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:01.080
playing at the same time.

285
00:12:01.080 --> 00:12:02.440
So you could have multiple animations

286
00:12:02.440 --> 00:12:05.889
laid on top of one another playing at the same time.

287
00:12:05.889 --> 00:12:07.330
But what I'm going to do is just delete

288
00:12:07.330 --> 00:12:09.600
that second animation track.

289
00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:11.519
And I'm going to zoom out a little bit

290
00:12:11.519 --> 00:12:14.120
because our animation is actually longer

291
00:12:14.120 --> 00:12:16.039
than our timeline here.

292
00:12:16.039 --> 00:12:17.620
So we can remedy that quite easily

293
00:12:17.620 --> 00:12:19.559
by just grabbing this end point

294
00:12:19.559 --> 00:12:22.059
and just dragging it along a bit.

295
00:12:22.059 --> 00:12:24.179
So now if I hit space bar,

296
00:12:24.179 --> 00:12:25.220
you can see that our animation

297
00:12:25.220 --> 00:12:28.820
is playing on our character here.

298
00:12:28.820 --> 00:12:31.840
Now, if we want to create a new animation,

299
00:12:31.840 --> 00:12:35.320
say for example, we want to create like a walk start,

300
00:12:35.320 --> 00:12:36.980
what I could do here,

301
00:12:36.980 --> 00:12:39.879
if I scrub past the end of this animation

302
00:12:39.879 --> 00:12:42.759
and then hit the plus on the animation track,

303
00:12:42.759 --> 00:12:46.460
we could search for a walk forward.

304
00:12:46.460 --> 00:12:48.179
And then once we scrub over this,

305
00:12:48.179 --> 00:12:52.120
we can see that this plays after our idle.

306
00:12:52.120 --> 00:12:55.519
So I can actually blend these two animations together

307
00:12:55.519 --> 00:12:58.220
by just overlapping the two animations.

308
00:12:58.220 --> 00:13:00.740
I zoom in a little bit to get a bit better control

309
00:13:00.740 --> 00:13:02.480
by just overlapping these animations.

310
00:13:02.480 --> 00:13:04.360
And you can see that automatically

311
00:13:04.360 --> 00:13:08.460
we get a blend created.

312
00:13:08.460 --> 00:13:10.860
Now, when we scrub over this,

313
00:13:10.860 --> 00:13:12.899
you can see that it blends from the idle

314
00:13:13.460 --> 00:13:14.620
into that walk forward.

315
00:13:14.620 --> 00:13:16.940
And it gives us a sort of walk start

316
00:13:16.940 --> 00:13:18.600
or at least something where you can start off

317
00:13:18.600 --> 00:13:21.379
with a base of animation and then tweak it

318
00:13:21.379 --> 00:13:27.620
if you wanted to, to get something a little nicer.

319
00:13:27.620 --> 00:13:29.620
We could then bake this animation out

320
00:13:29.620 --> 00:13:32.100
to its own animation sequence.

321
00:13:32.100 --> 00:13:33.299
And you can see quite quickly,

322
00:13:33.299 --> 00:13:39.929
we've created just a new animation that is a walk start.

323
00:13:39.929 --> 00:13:42.210
Well, what if we wanted to add a bit more

324
00:13:42.210 --> 00:13:44.149
to this animation that wasn't just using

325
00:13:44.149 --> 00:13:46.429
pre-existing animations in the project?

326
00:13:46.470 --> 00:13:49.629
So you can use something called a layered control rig.

327
00:13:49.629 --> 00:13:51.169
Now, this is something that came in

328
00:13:51.169 --> 00:13:54.169
with version 5.4 of the engine.

329
00:13:54.169 --> 00:13:57.350
And it works in a similar way to animation layers.

330
00:13:57.350 --> 00:13:59.029
So if you've used those before,

331
00:13:59.029 --> 00:14:02.139
then this should be quite straightforward.

332
00:14:02.139 --> 00:14:03.340
So on our character,

333
00:14:04.220 --> 00:14:06.179
I can choose the plus icon here,

334
00:14:06.179 --> 00:14:07.620
go to control rig,

335
00:14:07.620 --> 00:14:10.659
and I'll take the layered option at the top here.

336
00:14:10.659 --> 00:14:12.379
And this will show us all the assets,

337
00:14:12.379 --> 00:14:13.860
all of the control rigs that are available

338
00:14:13.860 --> 00:14:17.129
for this character that have the layered feature.

339
00:14:17.129 --> 00:14:18.929
So as soon as I select that,

340
00:14:20.179 --> 00:14:21.000
that'll bring that in.

341
00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:24.460
And if you notice, compared to our previous control rig,

342
00:14:24.460 --> 00:14:25.620
the layered control rig track

343
00:14:25.620 --> 00:14:27.019
is actually a different color.

344
00:14:27.019 --> 00:14:28.299
So it's quite a good indicator

345
00:14:28.299 --> 00:14:32.500
of which kind of control rig you have.

346
00:14:32.500 --> 00:14:34.340
So if I scrub through this now,

347
00:14:34.340 --> 00:14:37.139
you can see that our animation is playing

348
00:14:37.139 --> 00:14:41.080
as well as our control rig being available.

349
00:14:41.080 --> 00:14:43.659
So we can do the same sort of thing here.

350
00:14:43.659 --> 00:14:46.500
Let's just add a wave to the beginning like we did before.

351
00:14:46.500 --> 00:14:51.980
So if I hit the plus and then scrub along a little bit,

352
00:14:51.980 --> 00:14:54.830
go back into local mode for this,

353
00:14:54.830 --> 00:14:59.850
very briefly just bring these guys up.

354
00:14:59.850 --> 00:15:03.019
I mean, you can already see if I hit play

355
00:15:03.019 --> 00:15:06.059
that our animation is still playing underneath

356
00:15:06.059 --> 00:15:08.230
and our new pose on top

357
00:15:08.230 --> 00:15:16.720
is working essentially like an animation layer.

358
00:15:16.720 --> 00:15:23.340
So if I finish off the rest of the wave,

359
00:15:23.379 --> 00:15:25.659
I'm going to go back into world mode there.

360
00:15:25.659 --> 00:15:27.779
Oh, that's maybe not so useful.

361
00:15:27.779 --> 00:15:39.549
Just control Z there.

362
00:15:39.549 --> 00:15:46.539
And we'll just very quickly add a wave to our character.

363
00:15:46.539 --> 00:15:47.899
And I'm going to do the same thing again,

364
00:15:47.899 --> 00:15:51.450
where I selected all of these controls,

365
00:15:51.450 --> 00:15:54.509
press S to set a key,

366
00:15:54.509 --> 00:15:56.070
copy the first key,

367
00:15:56.070 --> 00:16:01.580
and then paste it again to return to our idle pose.

368
00:16:01.899 --> 00:16:05.679
So if I control C, control V the clavicle as well.

369
00:16:05.679 --> 00:16:09.710
So now if I just zoom out a little bit,

370
00:16:09.710 --> 00:16:10.990
when our animation plays,

371
00:16:10.990 --> 00:16:14.509
you can see that we've added this extra wave on top of it.

372
00:16:14.509 --> 00:16:17.750
And then we revert back to the existing animation

373
00:16:17.750 --> 00:16:21.889
and it goes into the walk start that we made before.

374
00:16:21.889 --> 00:16:23.210
And we can do the same thing with this.

375
00:16:23.210 --> 00:16:25.659
If I go back to the top,

376
00:16:25.659 --> 00:16:28.620
if we scrub to the end of the time slider here,

377
00:16:28.620 --> 00:16:30.220
press this little red button

378
00:16:30.220 --> 00:16:31.740
that'll set our playback range,

379
00:16:31.740 --> 00:16:33.139
the end of our playback range

380
00:16:33.139 --> 00:16:35.639
to where our time slider is.

381
00:16:35.639 --> 00:16:38.779
And I can right click,

382
00:16:38.779 --> 00:16:41.659
bake our animation sequence,

383
00:16:41.659 --> 00:16:49.600
call that A underscore wave walk,

384
00:16:49.600 --> 00:16:53.049
hit export to animation sequence.

385
00:16:53.049 --> 00:16:56.610
And again, that's our wave walk just here.

386
00:16:56.610 --> 00:16:59.299
So you can see that our character's waving,

387
00:16:59.299 --> 00:17:02.340
they have the idle animation working underneath it,

388
00:17:02.340 --> 00:17:04.200
and then they blend into the walk.
